window.LB = window.LB || {};
LB["p5-electric"] = {
  notes: [
    { h: "Parts of an electric circuit", t: "A simple circuit is made of a battery (or cell), wires, a bulb and often a switch. The battery is the source of electrical energy, the wires carry the electric current, and the bulb changes electrical energy into light and heat energy. A battery is made of one or more cells.", kw: ["source of electrical energy", "electric current", "battery", "cell"] },
    { h: "Closed and open circuits", t: "Electric current flows only in a closed circuit, which is a complete, unbroken path from one end of the battery, through the parts, and back to the other end. If there is a break anywhere, such as an open switch, a loose wire or a fused bulb, the circuit is an open circuit and no current flows, so the bulb does not light up.", kw: ["closed circuit", "open circuit", "complete, unbroken path", "no current flows"] },
    { h: "What a switch does", t: "A switch is used to open or close a circuit. When the switch is closed (on), the circuit is closed and current flows. When the switch is open (off), there is a gap in the circuit, so it becomes an open circuit and current stops flowing.", kw: ["open or close a circuit", "switch is closed", "switch is open"] },
    { h: "How a bulb lights up", t: "Inside a bulb is a thin coiled wire called the filament. When current flows through the filament, it gets very hot and glows. A bulb fuses when its filament breaks; this leaves a gap, so current cannot flow through that bulb.", kw: ["filament", "fuses", "gap"] },
    { h: "Circuit symbols in words", t: "Circuit diagrams use symbols. A cell is drawn as a long thin line and a short thick line side by side; a battery is two or more cells joined together. A bulb is a circle with a cross inside, a wire is a straight line, and an open switch is a line with a break and a tilted line (a closed switch has no break).", kw: ["long thin line and a short thick line", "circle with a cross", "straight line", "switch"] },
    { h: "Electrical conductors and insulators", t: "An electrical conductor allows electric current to flow through it; metals such as copper, iron, steel and aluminium, and graphite (pencil lead), are conductors. An electrical insulator does not allow electric current to flow through it; plastic, rubber, wood, glass, cloth and paper are insulators. Wires are made of copper covered with plastic or rubber.", kw: ["electrical conductor", "allows electric current to flow through", "electrical insulator", "does not allow electric current"] },
    { h: "Testing objects across a gap", t: "To test a material, place the object across the gap between two crocodile clips in a circuit. If the bulb lights up, the object is a conductor and the circuit is closed. If the bulb does not light up, the object is an insulator and the circuit stays open. First check that the circuit works by joining the clips together.", kw: ["across the gap", "lights up", "conductor", "insulator"] },
    { h: "Series circuits", t: "In a series circuit the parts are joined one after another, so there is only one path for the current. If one bulb fuses, the circuit becomes open and all the bulbs go out. Adding more bulbs in series makes each bulb dimmer; adding more batteries in series makes the bulbs brighter.", kw: ["only one path", "all the bulbs go out", "more bulbs in series", "dimmer", "more batteries in series", "brighter"] },
    { h: "Parallel circuits", t: "In a parallel circuit there is more than one path for the current, and each bulb is on its own path (branch). If one bulb fuses, current still flows through the other paths, so the other bulbs stay lit. With identical bulbs and the same battery, each bulb in parallel is as bright as a single bulb connected to that battery.", kw: ["more than one path", "own path", "stay lit", "as bright as a single bulb"] },
    { h: "Which switch controls which bulb?", t: "Trace every path from the battery and back. A switch on the main path (where all current passes) controls all the bulbs. A switch on a branch controls only the bulb(s) on that same branch. A bulb lights only if there is a closed path through it back to the battery.", kw: ["main path", "controls all the bulbs", "same branch", "closed path"] },
    { h: "Electromagnets", t: "Coil an insulated wire around an iron nail and connect it to a battery. When current flows, the iron nail becomes a magnet called an electromagnet. More coils of wire or more batteries make the electromagnet stronger, so it attracts more paper clips. When the current is switched off, the iron nail loses its magnetism, so the electromagnet can be switched on and off.", kw: ["electromagnet", "more coils", "more batteries", "stronger", "switched off", "loses its magnetism"] },
    { h: "Electrical safety and saving electricity", t: "Never touch switches or plugs with wet hands, because water can conduct electricity and you may get an electric shock. Do not overload a socket, and do not use appliances with damaged wires. Save electricity by switching off lights, fans and appliances when not in use, using energy-saving (LED) bulbs, and setting the air-conditioner at about 25 °C; this saves money and resources.", kw: ["electric shock", "wet hands", "switching off", "energy-saving", "saves money"] }
  ],
  traps: [
    "A circuit with a battery and a bulb always lights up -> The bulb lights ONLY in a closed circuit; any gap (open switch, loose wire, fused bulb) stops the current.",
    "All metals are magnetic, so only magnetic things conduct -> All metals conduct electricity, including aluminium and copper, which are NOT magnetic.",
    "Only metals conduct electricity -> Graphite (pencil lead) is not a metal but is an electrical conductor.",
    "If one bulb fuses, all bulbs always go out -> That is only true in a SERIES circuit; in parallel the bulbs on other paths stay lit.",
    "More bulbs in series make the circuit brighter -> More bulbs in series make EACH bulb DIMMER; more batteries in series make them brighter.",
    "Bulbs in parallel share the battery, so they are dimmer -> With identical bulbs, each bulb in parallel is as bright as a single bulb on the same battery.",
    "A switch anywhere controls every bulb -> A switch on a branch controls only the bulbs on that branch; only a switch on the main path controls all of them.",
    "An electromagnet stays magnetic after it is switched off -> The iron nail loses its magnetism when no current flows, which is why electromagnets can be switched off."
  ],
  lessons: [
    {
      h: "Tracing current: which bulbs light up?",
      concept: "A bulb lights only if there is a closed path from the battery, through that bulb, and back to the battery. Check the main path first: if a switch there is open, NO bulb lights. Then check each branch: a bulb on a branch lights only if every switch on its branch is closed.",
      example: {
        q: "Study the circuit. Switch S1 is closed, S2 is closed and S3 is open. Which bulbs light up? Explain.",
        fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "switch", label: "S1", open: false }], branches: [[{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "switch", label: "S2", open: false }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "B" }, { k: "switch", label: "S3", open: true }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "C" }]] },
        marks: 2,
        think: [
          "Step 1: The main path has S1, which is closed, so current can leave and return to the battery.",
          "Step 2: Branch with A: S2 is closed, so this path is a closed circuit and A lights up.",
          "Step 3: Branch with B: S3 is open, so there is a gap and no current flows through B.",
          "Step 4: Branch with C has no switch, so it is a closed path and C lights up."
        ],
        answer: "Bulbs A and C light up. S1 on the main path is closed, and the paths through A and C are closed circuits, so current flows through them. S3 is open, so the path through B is an open circuit and B does not light up."
      },
      tip: "Put your finger on the battery and trace each path round. Any open switch or gap on that path means ‘no current here’.",
      try: {
        q: "In the circuit shown, S1 is open while S2 and S3 are closed. Which bulbs light up?",
        fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "switch", label: "S1", open: true }], branches: [[{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "switch", label: "S2", open: false }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "B" }, { k: "switch", label: "S3", open: false }]] },
        o: ["Bulb A only", "Bulb B only", "Both A and B", "Neither A nor B"],
        a: 3,
        why: "S1 is on the main path, which all the current must pass through. With S1 open the whole circuit is open, so neither bulb lights even though S2 and S3 are closed."
      }
    },
    {
      h: "One bulb fuses: series or parallel?",
      concept: "In a series circuit there is only one path, so a fused bulb makes a gap in that path and ALL bulbs go out. In a parallel circuit each bulb has its own path, so when one bulb fuses the others still have a closed path and stay lit. Bulbs on the SAME branch as the fused bulb go out, because they share its path.",
      example: {
        q: "In the circuit shown, bulb A fuses. State which bulbs still light up and explain why.",
        fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, branches: [[{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "bulb", label: "B" }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "C" }]] },
        marks: 2,
        think: [
          "Step 1: A and B are on the same branch, joined in series with each other.",
          "Step 2: When A fuses, there is a gap in the path through A and B, so no current flows through B either.",
          "Step 3: C is on a separate path, which is still a closed circuit, so C stays lit."
        ],
        answer: "Only bulb C lights up. A and B are on the same path, so when A fuses that path becomes an open circuit and B goes out. C is on a separate path that is still a closed circuit, so current still flows through C."
      },
      tip: "Always say WHICH path is broken and WHICH path is still a closed circuit.",
      try: {
        q: "Three bulbs are connected as shown. Bulb B fuses. What happens to bulbs A and C?",
        fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "bulb", label: "B" }, { k: "bulb", label: "C" }] },
        o: ["A and C stay lit", "A and C go out", "Only A goes out", "A and C become brighter"],
        a: 1,
        why: "The bulbs are in series, so there is only one path. When B fuses, the circuit becomes open and no current flows, so A and C go out too."
      }
    },
    {
      h: "Predicting brightness",
      concept: "Assume the bulbs and batteries are identical. In series: more batteries in series make the bulbs brighter; more bulbs in series make each bulb dimmer. In parallel: each bulb on its own path is as bright as a single bulb connected to the same battery. Two bulbs in series on one branch are dimmer than one bulb alone on another branch.",
      example: {
        q: "Study the circuit, which has two batteries. Compare the brightness of bulb A with bulb B, and of bulb B with bulb C.",
        fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 2, branches: [[{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "B" }, { k: "bulb", label: "C" }]] },
        marks: 2,
        think: [
          "Step 1: Each branch acts like its own circuit connected to the two batteries.",
          "Step 2: A is the only bulb on its branch, like one bulb with two batteries.",
          "Step 3: B and C are two bulbs in series on the other branch, so each is dimmer than A.",
          "Step 4: B and C are on the same path and identical, so they are equally bright."
        ],
        answer: "Bulb A is brighter than bulb B, because B shares its path with another bulb in series while A is alone on its path. B and C are equally bright because they are identical bulbs in series on the same path."
      },
      tip: "Look at ONE path at a time and count batteries and bulbs on it.",
      try: {
        q: "The circuit shown has two batteries and two bulbs in series. How bright is each bulb compared with ONE bulb connected to ONE battery?",
        fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 2, main: [{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "bulb", label: "B" }] },
        o: ["Brighter", "Dimmer", "As bright", "It does not light up"],
        a: 2,
        why: "Doubling the batteries makes the bulbs brighter, but doubling the bulbs in series makes them dimmer by the same amount, so each bulb is about as bright as one bulb with one battery."
      }
    },
    {
      h: "Conductor or insulator? Explaining the gap test",
      concept: "In the gap test the object becomes part of the circuit. A full-mark explanation links THREE ideas: the material (conductor or insulator), whether it allows electric current to flow through, and whether the circuit is closed or open. Remember that all metals conduct (even non-magnetic ones like aluminium and copper), and graphite also conducts.",
      example: {
        q: "A plastic ruler is placed across gap X. Explain why the bulb does not light up.",
        fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "gap", label: "X", text: "plastic ruler" }] },
        marks: 2,
        think: [
          "Step 1: Name the material property: plastic is an electrical insulator.",
          "Step 2: Say what an insulator does: it does not allow electric current to flow through it.",
          "Step 3: Link to the circuit: the circuit is open, so no current flows through the bulb."
        ],
        answer: "Plastic is an electrical insulator, so it does not allow electric current to flow through it. The circuit is an open circuit, so no current flows through the bulb and it does not light up."
      },
      tip: "‘Insulator’ alone is not enough. Add ‘does not allow current to flow through’ and ‘open circuit’.",
      try: {
        q: "Which object, placed across gap X, will make bulb A light up?",
        fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "gap", label: "X", text: "?" }] },
        o: ["A rubber band", "A wooden ice-cream stick", "A strip of aluminium foil", "A glass rod"],
        a: 2,
        why: "Aluminium is a metal and an electrical conductor, so it closes the circuit. Aluminium is not magnetic, but that does not matter: all metals conduct electricity."
      }
    },
    {
      h: "Electromagnet experiments and graphs",
      concept: "The strength of an electromagnet is measured by the number of paper clips it attracts. To test one factor (number of coils OR number of batteries), change only that factor and keep everything else the same: the nail, the size and type of paper clips, the other factor. State the trend from the graph using ‘the more …, the more …’.",
      example: {
        q: "Study the bar graph from an electromagnet experiment in which only the number of coils was changed. (a) State the relationship shown. (b) Predict the number of clips for 50 coils.",
        fig: { type: "bar", title: "Electromagnet strength", xl: "Number of coils", yl: "Number of paper clips attracted", bars: [["10", 3], ["20", 6], ["30", 9], ["40", 12]] },
        marks: 2,
        think: [
          "Step 1: Read the bars: each extra 10 coils adds 3 paper clips.",
          "Step 2: State the trend: the more coils, the more clips, so the stronger the electromagnet.",
          "Step 3: Continue the pattern: 40 coils gives 12, so 50 coils gives 15."
        ],
        answer: "(a) The greater the number of coils, the greater the number of paper clips attracted, so the stronger the electromagnet. (b) 15 paper clips."
      },
      tip: "Strength is shown by clips. Write ‘stronger electromagnet’, not just ‘more clips’.",
      try: {
        q: "A pupil wants to find out if the number of batteries affects the strength of an electromagnet. Which must she change?",
        o: ["The number of coils only", "The number of batteries only", "The number of batteries and the number of coils", "The type of nail only"],
        a: 1,
        why: "In a fair test only the factor being tested (number of batteries) is changed; the coils, nail and paper clips are kept the same."
      }
    }
  ],
  mcq: [
    { q: "Which of these is an electrical conductor?", o: ["Rubber band", "Copper wire", "Plastic straw", "Glass marble"], a: 1, why: "Copper is a metal, and metals are electrical conductors. Rubber, plastic and glass are insulators.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "Electric current can flow in a circuit only when the circuit is", o: ["closed", "open", "broken at one point", "without a switch"], a: 0, why: "Current flows only in a closed circuit, which is a complete path. A circuit without a switch can still work, but only if it is closed.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "Study the circuit. Why does bulb A not light up?", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "switch", label: "S", open: true }] }, o: ["Switch S is open, so the circuit is an open circuit.", "The bulb is too far from the battery for current to reach it.", "The wires are electrical insulators, so no current flows.", "There is only one bulb, so the circuit is not complete."], a: 0, why: "With S open there is a gap, so the circuit is open and no current flows. The wires are conductors.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "Which part of a circuit is the source of electrical energy?", o: ["Battery", "Bulb", "Switch", "Wire"], a: 0, why: "The battery supplies electrical energy. The bulb uses it, the wire carries current and the switch opens or closes the circuit.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "A steel spoon is placed across gap X in the circuit shown. What is observed?", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "gap", label: "X", text: "steel spoon" }] }, o: ["Bulb A does not light up because steel is an insulator.", "Bulb A lights up only if the spoon is wet.", "Bulb A flickers and then fuses.", "Bulb A lights up because steel is an electrical conductor."], a: 3, why: "Steel is a metal and conducts electricity, so the circuit is closed and A lights up.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "What is the use of a switch in a circuit?", o: ["To store electrical energy", "To open or close the circuit", "To make the bulb brighter", "To change electrical energy into light energy"], a: 1, why: "A switch opens (breaks) or closes (completes) the circuit, turning the current off or on.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "To make an electromagnet, an insulated wire is coiled around a nail made of", o: ["iron", "copper", "plastic", "wood"], a: 0, why: "Iron is a magnetic material, so the nail becomes a magnet when current flows through the coil. Copper, plastic and wood are not magnetic.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "Why should we never touch a switch with wet hands?", o: ["Water can conduct electricity, so we may get an electric shock.", "Water makes the metal switch rust at once, so it breaks.", "Water is an electrical insulator, so it stops the switch working.", "Water makes the bulb fuse as soon as the switch is touched."], a: 0, why: "Tap water conducts electricity, so current could pass through our body and give an electric shock.", lvl: 1 },

    { q: "The two bulbs in the circuit shown are lit. Bulb A then fuses. What happens to bulb B?", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "bulb", label: "B" }] }, o: ["B becomes brighter.", "B stays lit.", "B becomes dimmer but stays lit.", "B goes out."], a: 3, why: "A and B are in series, so there is only one path. The fused bulb makes a gap, the circuit becomes open, and B goes out.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Both bulbs in the circuit shown are lit. Bulb A then fuses. What happens to bulb B?", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, branches: [[{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "B" }]] }, o: ["B stays lit and is as bright as before.", "B goes out because the circuit is broken.", "B becomes brighter as it gets all the current.", "B becomes dimmer because A no longer helps it."], a: 0, why: "A and B are in parallel. B is on its own path, which is still a closed circuit, so B stays lit with the same brightness.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Study the circuit. Which change will make both bulbs brighter?", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "bulb", label: "B" }] }, o: ["Add another bulb in series", "Add another battery in series", "Replace one wire with a cotton string", "Add an open switch to the circuit"], a: 1, why: "More batteries in series make the bulbs brighter. Another bulb in series makes them dimmer; a string or open switch opens the circuit.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Study the circuit. Which change will make the bulbs that remain lit brighter?", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "bulb", label: "B" }, { k: "bulb", label: "C" }] }, o: ["Remove bulb C and join the wires together", "Add a fourth bulb in series", "Replace a copper wire with a plastic strip", "Swap the positions of bulbs A and C"], a: 0, why: "Fewer bulbs in series make each bulb brighter. Swapping positions changes nothing; a plastic strip opens the circuit.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Study the circuit. S1 is closed and S2 is open. Which bulb(s) light up?", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "switch", label: "S1", open: false }], branches: [[{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "switch", label: "S2", open: true }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "B" }]] }, o: ["A only", "A and B", "B only", "Neither A nor B"], a: 2, why: "S1 on the main path is closed. The branch with A has S2 open, so no current flows through A; the branch with B is closed, so B lights up.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Objects P, Q, R and S were each placed across the gap between two crocodile clips in a working circuit. The results are shown. Which objects are electrical conductors?", tbl: [["Object", "Did the bulb light up?"], ["P", "Yes"], ["Q", "No"], ["R", "Yes"], ["S", "No"]], o: ["P and Q only", "Q and S only", "P, Q and R only", "P and R only"], a: 3, why: "The bulb lights up only when the object is a conductor that closes the circuit, so P and R are conductors.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Study the bar graph. The electromagnet used the same nail and battery each time. Predict the number of paper clips it will attract with 40 coils.", fig: { type: "bar", xl: "Number of coils", yl: "Number of paper clips attracted", bars: [["10", 4], ["20", 8], ["30", 12]] }, o: ["12", "16", "4", "20"], a: 1, why: "Every extra 10 coils adds 4 paper clips, so 40 coils should attract about 16 clips.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "In a circuit diagram, how is a cell drawn?", o: ["A circle with a cross inside it", "A straight line with no breaks in it", "A long thin line beside a short thick line", "A line with a break and a tilted line"], a: 2, why: "A cell is a long thin line and a short thick line. A circle with a cross is a bulb, a straight line is a wire and a broken line with a tilted line is an open switch.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Which habit helps to save electricity at home?", o: ["Leaving the TV on standby all night", "Setting the air-conditioner to 18 °C", "Opening the fridge door often to check the food", "Switching off lights and fans when leaving a room"], a: 3, why: "Switching off appliances when not in use saves electricity. A very low air-con setting and opening the fridge often waste electricity.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "A wooden chopstick is placed across gap X and bulb A does not light up. Which object, used instead of the chopstick, will make bulb A light up?", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "gap", label: "X", text: "wooden chopstick" }] }, o: ["A steel paper clip", "A plastic ruler", "A rubber band", "A cotton thread"], a: 0, why: "Steel is a metal and conducts electricity, so it closes the circuit. Plastic, rubber and cotton are insulators like wood.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Why are the copper wires in electrical cables covered with plastic?", o: ["Plastic is a conductor, so current flows faster.", "Plastic is an insulator, so it prevents electric shocks.", "Plastic makes the wire magnetic, so current flows better.", "Plastic makes the bulb brighter by keeping heat in."], a: 1, why: "Plastic is an electrical insulator; it does not allow current to flow through it, so touching the cable is safe.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Study the circuit. All the bulbs are identical. How bright is each bulb compared with one bulb connected alone to the same battery?", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, branches: [[{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "B" }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "C" }]] }, o: ["Dimmer", "Brighter", "As bright", "Only A is as bright; B and C are dimmer"], a: 2, why: "The bulbs are in parallel. Each bulb is on its own path to the battery, so each is as bright as a single bulb on that battery.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "An electromagnet is holding some paper clips. When the switch is opened, the clips drop. Why?", o: ["The nail becomes heavier, so the clips slide off.", "The paper clips become magnets and repel one another.", "The wire becomes an insulator when the switch is opened.", "No current flows, so the iron nail loses its magnetism."], a: 3, why: "An electromagnet is a magnet only while current flows. When the circuit is opened, the iron nail loses its magnetism and the clips fall.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Study the circuit. What happens when switch S is closed?", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "switch", label: "S", open: true }, { k: "bulb", label: "B" }] }, o: ["Only A lights up.", "Only B lights up.", "Neither bulb lights up.", "Both A and B light up."], a: 3, why: "A, S and B are in series on one path. Closing S closes the circuit, so current flows through both bulbs.", lvl: 2 },

    { q: "Study the circuit. S1 and S2 are closed; S3 is open. Which bulbs light up?", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "switch", label: "S1", open: false }], branches: [[{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "switch", label: "S2", open: false }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "B" }, { k: "switch", label: "S3", open: true }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "C" }]] }, o: ["A only", "B and C only", "A and C only", "A, B and C"], a: 2, why: "S1 on the main path is closed. The paths through A (S2 closed) and C (no switch) are closed; the path through B has S3 open, so B does not light.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Study the circuit. Switch S is open. Which bulbs light up?", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }], branches: [[{ k: "bulb", label: "B" }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "C" }, { k: "switch", label: "S", open: true }]] }, o: ["A and B only", "B only", "A and C only", "A, B and C"], a: 0, why: "A is on the main path. Current flows through A and then the closed branch through B. The branch with C is open, so C does not light.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Study the circuit. All the switches are open. Which switches must be closed so that ONLY bulb B lights up?", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "switch", label: "S1", open: true }], branches: [[{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "switch", label: "S2", open: true }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "B" }, { k: "switch", label: "S3", open: true }]] }, o: ["S3 only", "S1 and S2 only", "S1, S2 and S3", "S1 and S3 only"], a: 3, why: "S1 on the main path must be closed for any bulb to light, and S3 closes B’s branch. S2 must stay open so that A stays off.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Each object in the table was placed across gap X. Which objects will make bulb A light up?", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "gap", label: "X", text: "object" }] }, tbl: [["Object", "Material"], ["P", "aluminium foil"], ["Q", "plastic comb"], ["R", "pencil lead (graphite)"], ["S", "rubber eraser"]], o: ["P only", "P and R only", "Q and S only", "P, Q and R only"], a: 1, why: "Aluminium (a metal) and graphite both conduct electricity, so they close the circuit. Plastic and rubber are insulators.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "The table shows four electromagnets. Which two should be compared to find out if the number of coils affects the strength of an electromagnet?", tbl: [["Set-up", "Number of coils", "Number of batteries", "Nail"], ["A", "20", "1", "iron"], ["B", "40", "1", "iron"], ["C", "40", "2", "iron"], ["D", "20", "2", "steel"]], o: ["A and B", "A and C", "B and C", "C and D"], a: 0, why: "A and B differ only in the number of coils. A and C also differ in batteries, B and C differ only in batteries, and C and D differ in coils and nail.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "All three bulbs in the circuit are lit. Bulb A then fuses. Which bulb(s) still light up?", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, branches: [[{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "bulb", label: "B" }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "C" }]] }, o: ["B and C", "B only", "C only", "None of the bulbs"], a: 2, why: "A and B share one path, so when A fuses that path is open and B goes out too. C is on a separate closed path, so it stays lit.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Which statement about a series circuit with identical bulbs is NOT true?", o: ["There is only one path for the current.", "If one bulb fuses, all the bulbs go out.", "Adding more batteries in series makes the bulbs brighter.", "Adding more bulbs in series makes each bulb brighter."], a: 3, why: "Adding more bulbs in series makes each bulb dimmer, not brighter. The other three statements are true.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Four electromagnets W, X, Y and Z were made with the same iron nail and one battery each, but different numbers of coils. The bar graph shows the number of paper clips each attracted. Arrange them from MOST coils to FEWEST coils.", fig: { type: "bar", xl: "Electromagnet", yl: "Number of paper clips attracted", bars: [["W", 6], ["X", 14], ["Y", 2], ["Z", 10]] }, o: ["Y, W, Z, X", "X, Z, W, Y", "X, W, Z, Y", "Z, X, W, Y"], a: 1, why: "More coils make a stronger electromagnet that attracts more clips: X (14) > Z (10) > W (6) > Y (2).", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Study the circuit. S1 is open. Which bulbs light up?", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "switch", label: "S1", open: true }], branches: [[{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "B" }]] }, o: ["A only", "Both, because each bulb has its own path", "B only", "Neither, because S1 is on the main path"], a: 3, why: "All current must pass through the main path. With S1 open, neither branch has a closed path back to the battery.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "The bulbs in Mei Ling’s series circuit do not light up even though the switch is closed. Which could be the reason(s)? P: One bulb has fused. Q: The battery is used up. R: A wire has come loose. S: An extra battery was added in series.", o: ["P and Q only", "Q and R only", "P, Q and R only", "P, R and S only"], a: 2, why: "A fused bulb or a loose wire leaves a gap; a used-up battery gives no current. Adding a battery in series makes bulbs brighter, not go out.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Why are the lights and appliances in an HDB flat connected in parallel?", o: ["So that all of them switch on and off together with one switch", "So that each can be switched on and off on its own, even if one fails", "So that fewer wires are needed to connect all of them", "So that each bulb is dimmer and uses less electricity"], a: 1, why: "In parallel each appliance is on its own path with its own switch, so one can be off or faulty while the rest work.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Pencil lead is placed across gap X and bulb A lights up. What does this show?", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "gap", label: "X", text: "pencil lead" }] }, o: ["Pencil lead is a magnetic material.", "Pencil lead is an electrical insulator.", "Pencil lead is an electrical conductor.", "Pencil lead is a metal."], a: 2, why: "The bulb lights, so the circuit is closed and pencil lead (graphite) conducts electricity. Graphite is NOT a metal, so ‘conductor’ is the only conclusion the result supports.", lvl: 3 },

    { q: "Study the circuit. S1 and S3 are closed; S2 is open. Bulb B then fuses. Which bulb(s) still light up?", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "switch", label: "S1", open: false }], branches: [[{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "switch", label: "S2", open: true }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "B" }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "C" }, { k: "switch", label: "S3", open: false }]] }, o: ["A and C", "A only", "None of the bulbs", "C only"], a: 3, why: "A was never lit because S2 is open. B’s fused filament breaks only B’s path. C’s path (through S1 and closed S3) is still closed, so C stays lit.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "Study the circuit. All the bulbs are identical. Which statement is correct?", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 2, branches: [[{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "B" }, { k: "bulb", label: "C" }]] }, o: ["A is brighter than B, and B and C are equally bright.", "A, B and C are equally bright.", "B and C are each brighter than A.", "A is dimmer than B, and C is dimmer than B."], a: 0, why: "Each branch is its own path to the batteries. A is alone on its path; B and C are two bulbs in series on theirs, so each is dimmer than A and equally bright.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "An electromagnet made with an iron nail attracts 12 paper clips. The iron nail is replaced with an aluminium rod of the same size; the coils and battery are unchanged. What is the most likely result?", o: ["More clips are attracted, because aluminium conducts electricity.", "The same number of clips, because the coils and battery are unchanged.", "Far fewer or no clips are attracted, because aluminium is not a magnetic material.", "The clips stay attracted even after the switch is opened."], a: 2, why: "The core must be a magnetic material such as iron to become a strong magnet. Aluminium conducts electricity but is not magnetic, so it does not become a magnet.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "Study the circuit. All the switches are open. Which switches must be closed so that bulbs A and C light up but bulb B does not?", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "switch", label: "S1", open: true }], branches: [[{ k: "bulb", label: "B" }, { k: "switch", label: "S2", open: true }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "C" }, { k: "switch", label: "S3", open: true }]] }, o: ["S1 only", "S1 and S3 only", "S2 and S3 only", "S1, S2 and S3"], a: 1, why: "A lights only if the circuit is closed through some branch. Closing S1 and S3 gives a closed path through A and C; S2 stays open so B is off. S1 alone leaves both branches open, so even A stays off.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "Objects X, Y and Z were tested. Which set of materials matches the results?", tbl: [["Object", "Lights the bulb across the gap?", "Attracted by a magnet?"], ["X", "Yes", "No"], ["Y", "Yes", "Yes"], ["Z", "No", "No"]], o: ["X iron, Y copper, Z glass", "X steel, Y aluminium, Z plastic", "X copper, Y steel, Z wood", "X plastic, Y nickel, Z copper"], a: 2, why: "X conducts but is not magnetic (copper); Y conducts and is magnetic (steel); Z is an insulator and not magnetic (wood). Iron and steel are magnetic; aluminium is not; copper conducts.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "Circuit P has one battery and one bulb. Circuit Q, shown, has two batteries and two bulbs in series. All batteries and bulbs are identical. How does bulb A in Q compare with the bulb in P?", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 2, main: [{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "bulb", label: "B" }] }, o: ["A is brighter.", "A is dimmer.", "A is about as bright.", "A does not light up."], a: 2, why: "Doubling the batteries in series makes the bulbs brighter, but doubling the bulbs in series makes them dimmer by the same amount, so A is about as bright as the bulb in P.", lvl: 4 }
  ],
  tf: [
    { s: "Electric current flows only in a closed circuit.", a: true, why: "A closed circuit is a complete path; any gap stops the current." },
    { s: "All metals are electrical conductors.", a: true, why: "Metals such as copper, aluminium, iron and steel all conduct electricity." },
    { s: "Only magnetic materials can conduct electricity.", a: false, why: "Copper and aluminium are not magnetic but are good conductors." },
    { s: "Graphite (pencil lead) is an electrical conductor.", a: true, why: "Graphite is not a metal but it allows current to flow through it." },
    { s: "Rubber and plastic are good electrical conductors.", a: false, why: "They are insulators; that is why wires are covered with them." },
    { s: "In a series circuit, if one bulb fuses, the other bulbs stay lit.", a: false, why: "There is only one path, so the circuit becomes open and all bulbs go out." },
    { s: "In a parallel circuit, if one bulb fuses, the bulbs on the other paths stay lit.", a: true, why: "The other paths are still closed circuits." },
    { s: "Adding more bulbs in series makes each bulb brighter.", a: false, why: "More bulbs in series make each bulb dimmer." },
    { s: "Adding more batteries in series makes the bulbs in a series circuit brighter.", a: true, why: "More batteries in series push more current through the bulbs." },
    { s: "Identical bulbs connected in parallel to one battery are dimmer than a single bulb on that battery.", a: false, why: "Each bulb in parallel is as bright as a single bulb on the same battery." },
    { s: "A switch on the main path of a circuit controls all the bulbs.", a: true, why: "All the current passes through the main path." },
    { s: "A switch on one branch of a parallel circuit controls every bulb in the circuit.", a: false, why: "It controls only the bulbs on its own branch." },
    { s: "A bulb fuses when its filament breaks.", a: true, why: "The broken filament leaves a gap, so current cannot flow through the bulb." },
    { s: "A battery is made of one or more cells.", a: true, why: "Several cells joined together form a battery." },
    { s: "An electromagnet keeps its magnetism after the current is switched off.", a: false, why: "The iron nail loses its magnetism when no current flows." },
    { s: "More coils of wire around the iron nail make an electromagnet stronger.", a: true, why: "More coils make it attract more paper clips." },
    { s: "An aluminium rod works as well as an iron nail for making an electromagnet.", a: false, why: "Aluminium is not a magnetic material, so it does not become a magnet." },
    { s: "It is safe to touch a plug with wet hands as long as the switch is on.", a: false, why: "Water can conduct electricity, so you may get an electric shock." },
    { s: "A bulb changes electrical energy into light energy and heat energy.", a: true, why: "The filament glows and also becomes very hot." },
    { s: "A circuit with a battery, wires and a bulb always lights up.", a: false, why: "It lights only if the circuit is closed and the parts work (no loose wire, fused bulb or used-up battery)." }
  ],
  sort: [
    { title: "Sort these into electrical conductors and electrical insulators.", groups: ["Conductor", "Insulator"], items: [["copper wire", 0], ["aluminium foil", 0], ["steel paper clip", 0], ["pencil lead (graphite)", 0], ["iron nail", 0], ["plastic ruler", 1], ["rubber band", 1], ["wooden chopstick", 1], ["glass marble", 1], ["cotton string", 1]] },
    { title: "Sort these statements into series circuit or parallel circuit (identical bulbs).", groups: ["Series circuit", "Parallel circuit"], items: [["Only one path for the current", 0], ["One bulb fuses, all bulbs go out", 0], ["More bulbs added, each bulb dimmer", 0], ["All bulbs share one path", 0], ["More than one path for the current", 1], ["One bulb fuses, the others stay lit", 1], ["Each bulb as bright as a single bulb", 1], ["Each bulb can have its own switch", 1]] },
    { title: "Sort these habits into saves electricity / safe, or wastes electricity / unsafe.", groups: ["Saves electricity or safe", "Wastes electricity or unsafe"], items: [["Switch off fans when leaving the room", 0], ["Use LED bulbs", 0], ["Set the air-con at 25 °C", 0], ["Dry your hands before touching a switch", 0], ["Leave the TV on standby all night", 1], ["Plug many appliances into one socket", 1], ["Use a hairdryer with a damaged wire", 1], ["Keep the fridge door open while choosing food", 1]] }
  ],
  oe: [
    { q: "Study the circuit. Explain why bulb A does not light up.", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "switch", label: "S", open: true }] }, marks: 2, kw: [["open"], ["current"]], model: "Switch S is open, so the circuit is an open circuit. No electric current flows through bulb A, so it does not light up." },
    { q: "A plastic ruler is placed across gap X. (a) Does bulb A light up? (b) Explain your answer. (c) Name one object that could be placed across X to make bulb A light up.", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "gap", label: "X", text: "plastic ruler" }] }, marks: 3, kw: [["insulator"], ["open circuit", "not closed", "incomplete", "no current"], ["steel", "copper", "iron", "aluminium", "metal", "graphite", "pencil lead"]], model: "(a) No, bulb A does not light up. (b) Plastic is an electrical insulator, so it does not allow electric current to flow through it. The circuit is an open circuit, so no current flows through the bulb. (c) A steel paper clip (any metal object, e.g. a copper key)." },
    { q: "Bulbs A, B and C are lit. Bulb B then fuses. (a) What happens to bulbs A and C? (b) Explain why. (c) How should the bulbs be connected so that A and C stay lit when B fuses?", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "bulb", label: "B" }, { k: "bulb", label: "C" }] }, marks: 3, kw: [["go out", "not light", "off"], ["one path", "open circuit", "gap"], ["parallel"]], model: "(a) Bulbs A and C go out. (b) The bulbs are in series, so there is only one path for the current. The fused bulb leaves a gap, so the circuit becomes an open circuit and no current flows. (c) Connect the bulbs in parallel, so that each bulb is on its own path." },
    { q: "The three identical bulbs are lit. (a) How bright is bulb A compared with one bulb connected alone to the same battery? (b) Bulb B fuses. What happens to bulbs A and C? (c) Explain your answer to (b).", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, branches: [[{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "B" }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "C" }]] }, marks: 3, kw: [["as bright", "same"], ["stay lit", "remain lit", "still light", "still lit"], ["own path", "separate path", "different path"]], model: "(a) Bulb A is as bright as a single bulb on the same battery. (b) Bulbs A and C stay lit. (c) The bulbs are in parallel and each bulb is on its own path, so the paths through A and C are still closed circuits and current still flows through them." },
    { q: "Study the circuit. S1 is closed, S2 is open and S3 is closed. (a) Which bulb(s) light up? (b) Which switch should be opened to switch off bulb B only? (c) Explain why switch S1 controls both bulbs.", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "switch", label: "S1", open: false }], branches: [[{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "switch", label: "S2", open: true }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "B" }, { k: "switch", label: "S3", open: false }]] }, marks: 3, kw: [["b only", "only b", "only bulb b", "bulb b only"], ["s3"], ["main path", "main"]], model: "(a) Only bulb B lights up, because S2 is open so the path through A is open. (b) S3, because it is on the same branch as B. (c) S1 is on the main path that all the current passes through." },
    { q: "Study the bar graph from an electromagnet experiment. Only the number of coils was changed. (a) State the relationship between the number of coils and the strength of the electromagnet. (b) Predict the number of paper clips attracted with 50 coils. (c) The switch is then opened. What happens to the paper clips? (d) Explain your answer to (c).", fig: { type: "bar", title: "Electromagnet strength", xl: "Number of coils", yl: "Number of paper clips attracted", bars: [["10", 3], ["20", 6], ["30", 9], ["40", 12]] }, marks: 4, kw: [["more coils", "greater the number of coils", "number of coils increases", "more the coils"], ["15"], ["drop", "fall"], ["loses its magnetism", "loses magnetism", "no longer a magnet", "no current"]], model: "(a) The more coils there are, the stronger the electromagnet, because it attracts more paper clips. (b) 15 paper clips. (c) The paper clips drop. (d) When the switch is opened, no current flows, so the iron nail loses its magnetism." },
    { q: "Study the circuit. (a) State what happens to the brightness of the bulbs if a third identical bulb is added in series. (b) Suggest one way to make bulbs A and B brighter without removing any bulb.", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "bulb", label: "B" }] }, marks: 2, kw: [["dimmer", "less bright"], ["battery", "batteries", "cell", "parallel"]], model: "(a) Each bulb becomes dimmer. (b) Add another battery in series (more batteries in series make the bulbs brighter)." },
    { q: "Objects were placed across the gap between two crocodile clips in a working circuit. (a) Which property of materials was being tested? (b) Which material in the table is the most suitable to cover an electrical wire? Explain your choice.", tbl: [["Object", "Bulb lit?"], ["Steel spoon", "Yes"], ["Rubber eraser", "No"], ["Copper coin", "Yes"], ["Aluminium foil", "Yes"]], marks: 3, kw: [["conduct"], ["rubber"], ["insulator", "does not allow", "not conduct"]], model: "(a) Whether the material can conduct electricity (is an electrical conductor or insulator). (b) Rubber. Rubber is an electrical insulator, so it does not allow electric current to flow through it and protects people from electric shocks." },
    { q: "Explain why you should not use a hairdryer with wet hands.", marks: 2, kw: [["conduct"], ["shock"]], model: "Water can conduct electricity, so electric current may flow through your body and you may get an electric shock." },
    { q: "Give one way to save electricity at home and one reason why it is important to save electricity.", marks: 2, kw: [["switch off", "turn off", "led", "energy-saving", "energy saving", "25"], ["money", "bill", "resources", "fuel"]], model: "Switch off lights and fans when leaving a room (or use LED bulbs). Saving electricity saves money on the electricity bill and saves the resources used to generate electricity." },
    { q: "Study the circuit. Switch S is open. (a) Which bulbs light up? (b) S is then closed. What happens to bulb C? (c) With S closed, bulb A fuses. What happens to bulbs B and C? Explain.", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, main: [{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }], branches: [[{ k: "bulb", label: "B" }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "C" }, { k: "switch", label: "S", open: true }]] }, marks: 4, kw: [["a and b", "b and a"], ["c light", "c will light", "c also light", "c is lit", "c is now lit"], ["go out", "not light", "off"], ["main path", "main"]], model: "(a) Bulbs A and B light up; C does not because S is open. (b) Bulb C lights up because its path becomes a closed circuit. (c) B and C go out. A is on the main path that all the current must pass through, so when A fuses the whole circuit becomes an open circuit.", x: 1 },
    { q: "Study the circuit with two batteries and three identical bulbs. (a) Compare the brightness of bulb A and bulb C. (b) Bulb A fuses. Which bulb(s) still light up? (c) Explain your answer to (b).", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 2, branches: [[{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "bulb", label: "B" }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "C" }]] }, marks: 3, kw: [["c is brighter", "brighter than a", "a is dimmer", "dimmer than c"], ["only c", "c only", "only bulb c", "c still", "c stays", "c remains"], ["separate path", "own path", "different path"]], model: "(a) Bulb C is brighter than bulb A, because A shares its path with bulb B in series while C is alone on its path. (b) Only C still lights up; bulb C stays lit and B goes out. (c) A and B are on the same path, so that path becomes open, but C is on a separate path that is still a closed circuit.", x: 1 },
    { q: "Siti wants to find out whether the number of batteries affects the strength of an electromagnet. (a) What should she change? (b) State two variables she must keep the same. (c) How can she measure the strength of the electromagnet?", marks: 4, kw: [["number of batteries"], ["number of coils", "coils"], ["nail", "size of paper clip", "type of paper clip", "same paper clip", "same clips"], ["number of paper clips", "number of clips", "clips attracted", "paper clips attracted"]], model: "(a) The number of batteries. (b) The number of coils of wire, and the same iron nail (same size and type of paper clips). (c) Count the number of paper clips attracted by the electromagnet; the more clips, the stronger it is." },
    { q: "Object X is placed across the gap in the circuit and both bulbs light up. (a) What can you conclude about X? (b) Bulb B is then unscrewed. What happens to bulb A? (c) Explain your answer to (b). (d) Bulb B is screwed back in and X is replaced with a wooden peg. Which bulb(s) light up?", fig: { type: "circuit", cells: 1, branches: [[{ k: "bulb", label: "A" }, { k: "gap", label: "X", text: "object X" }], [{ k: "bulb", label: "B" }]] }, marks: 4, kw: [["conductor"], ["stays lit", "stay lit", "still lit", "still light", "remains lit"], ["separate path", "own path", "different path"], ["b only", "only b", "only bulb b"]], model: "(a) X is an electrical conductor, because the path through A and X is a closed circuit. (b) Bulb A stays lit. (c) A is on a separate path from B, which is still a closed circuit. (d) Only B lights up (bulb B only), because wood is an insulator so the path through A is open.", x: 1 }
  ],
  doc: [
    { q: "A rubber band is placed across the gap in a circuit and the bulb does not light up. Explain why.", marks: 2, answers: ["Rubber is an electrical insulator, so it does not allow electric current to flow through. The circuit is open, so no current flows through the bulb.", "The rubber band is not strong enough.", "Rubber is an insulator."], best: 0, why: "Answer 1 names the property, what it does to current, and the open circuit. Answer 2 gives a wrong idea. Answer 3 names ‘insulator’ but does not link it to current not flowing, so it scores at most 1 mark." },
    { q: "In a series circuit with two bulbs, one bulb fuses and the other goes out. Explain why.", marks: 2, answers: ["The other bulb is also spoilt.", "The bulbs are in series, so there is only one path. The fused bulb leaves a gap, so the circuit is open and no current flows.", "Because it is a series circuit."], best: 1, why: "Answer 2 gives ‘only one path’ and ‘open circuit, no current’. Answer 1 is wrong – the other bulb still works. Answer 3 just names the circuit without explaining." },
    { q: "Why are the lights in a home connected in parallel instead of in series?", marks: 2, answers: ["Parallel is better.", "So they are brighter and look nicer.", "Each light is on its own path, so it can be switched on and off separately, and the others still work if one bulb fuses."], best: 2, why: "Answer 3 gives ‘own path’ plus the benefit. Answer 1 is too vague. Answer 2 is not the reason – in parallel each bulb is as bright as a single bulb, not brighter." },
    { q: "Explain how adding more coils affects an electromagnet.", marks: 2, answers: ["More coils make the electromagnet stronger, so it attracts more paper clips.", "It becomes bigger.", "More coils give more electricity so the nail is heavier."], best: 0, why: "Answer 1 states ‘stronger’ and the evidence (more paper clips). Answer 2 does not mention strength. Answer 3 gives wrong science." },
    { q: "Why are copper wires covered with plastic?", marks: 2, answers: ["To make the wire look nice.", "Plastic is an electrical insulator, so it does not allow current to flow through it; this prevents electric shocks when we touch the wire.", "Plastic is a conductor."], best: 1, why: "Answer 2 names the insulator property and the safety reason. Answer 1 gives no science. Answer 3 is wrong – plastic is an insulator." }
  ]
};
